WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.393 --> 00:00:12.658
The carriage stopped in front of a modest-looking courthouse.

2
00:00:13.739 --> 00:00:18.121
Mr. Fogg, Passepartout, and Aouda were taken into a room with barred windows.

3
00:00:18.141 --> 00:00:23.944
"You will appear before Judge Obadiah at half past eight," said the policeman.

4
00:00:25.445 --> 00:00:30.820
After the policeman had left them and closed the door, Passepartout fell into a chair and exclaimed, "Why

5
00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:31.581
are we prisoners?"

6
00:00:33.361 --> 00:00:36.383
Trying to conceal her emotions, Aouda, said to Mr.

7
00:00:36.403 --> 00:00:39.024
Fogg, "Sir, you must leave me to my fate!

8
00:00:39.964 --> 00:00:42.705
You have been arrested because of me—because you saved me!"

9
00:00:43.906 --> 00:00:46.347
Phileas Fogg explained that this was impossible.

10
00:00:47.928 --> 00:00:51.529
It was unlikely that he would be arrested for having prevented a human sacrifice.

11
00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:57.252
He would not, in any event, abandon Aouda but would escort her to Hong Kong.

12
00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:00.762
"But the steamer leaves at noon!"

13
00:01:01.222 --> 00:01:02.943
observed Passepartout nervously.

14
00:01:02.963 --> 00:01:06.926
"And we shall be on board by noon," replied Fogg.

15
00:01:08.227 --> 00:01:12.029
He said it so positively that Passepartout couldn't help but be reassured.

16
00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:15.411
At half past eight, the door opened.

17
00:01:16.752 --> 00:01:19.334
The policeman reappeared and instructed them to follow.

18
00:01:20.675 --> 00:01:21.876
He led them to a courtroom.

19
00:01:23.117 --> 00:01:27.680
Mr. Fogg and his two companions entered and took their places at a desk opposite the judge.

20
00:01:29.056 --> 00:01:30.737
Passepartout was getting more nervous.

21
00:01:32.117 --> 00:01:36.739
On the clock over the judge's head, the hands seemed to be going around very, very quickly.

22
00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:41.941
Judge Obadiah was a fat, round man who wore an old, white wig.

23
00:01:41.961 --> 00:01:44.983
"The first case, Mr.

24
00:01:45.723 --> 00:01:47.584
Phileas Fogg," he said.

25
00:01:47.604 --> 00:01:50.645
"I am here," replied Mr.

26
00:01:50.665 --> 00:01:52.826
Fogg. "Passepartout?"

27
00:01:53.166 --> 00:01:53.946
asked the judge.

28
00:01:53.966 --> 00:01:56.807
"Present," responded Passepartout.

29
00:01:56.827 --> 00:01:59.325
"Good," said the judge.

30
00:01:59.345 --> 00:02:04.309
"Authorities have been searching for you for two days on the train from Bombay."

31
00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:09.112
A door swung open, and three Indian priests entered.

32
00:02:10.574 --> 00:02:14.557
The priests took their places in front of the judge, and the clerk of the court stood.

33
00:02:16.298 --> 00:02:21.282
In a loud voice, he read a complaint of sacrilege against Phileas Fogg and his servant, who were

34
00:02:21.322 --> 00:02:25.085
accused of having violated a place held sacred by the Brahmin religion.

35
00:02:25.105 --> 00:02:28.328
"You hear the charge?"

36
00:02:28.752 --> 00:02:29.492
asked the judge.

37
00:02:29.512 --> 00:02:32.073
"Yes, sir," replied Mr.

38
00:02:32.113 --> 00:02:35.135
Fogg, consulting his watch, "and I admit to it."

39
00:02:36.335 --> 00:02:37.156
"You admit it?"

40
00:02:37.616 --> 00:02:38.356
asked the judge.

41
00:02:38.376 --> 00:02:40.377
"Yes," said Fogg.

42
00:02:40.397 --> 00:02:45.819
"And I wish to hear these priests admit to what they were going to do at the pagoda."

43
00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:50.901
The priests looked at each other, not seeming to understand what he was saying.

44
00:02:50.921 --> 00:02:55.023
"At the pagoda of Pillaji," said Passepartout.

45
00:02:55.823 --> 00:02:57.784
"They were on the point of burning their victim."

46
00:02:59.176 --> 00:03:02.318
The judge stared with astonishment, and the priests were shocked.

47
00:03:04.039 --> 00:03:04.860
"What victim?"

48
00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:06.120
asked the judge.

49
00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:10.423
"We are talking about the pagoda on Malabar Hill, in Bombay."

50
00:03:12.184 --> 00:03:14.465
The judge placed a pair of shoes on his desk.

51
00:03:14.485 --> 00:03:16.626
"My shoes!"

52
00:03:17.026 --> 00:03:18.607
cried Passepartout in surprise.

53
00:03:20.408 --> 00:03:25.111
In all the confusion since they had left Bombay, Phileas Fogg and his servant had quite forgotten the

54
00:03:25.171 --> 00:03:26.852
affair over Passepartout's shoes.

55
00:03:28.088 --> 00:03:32.551
They also did not notice the detective, Fix, watching from a corner of the courtroom.

56
00:03:34.232 --> 00:03:37.574
Fix had foreseen how Passepartout's misadventure could detain Mr.

57
00:03:37.614 --> 00:03:38.494
Fogg in Calcutta.

58
00:03:39.755 --> 00:03:44.158
He had gone to talk with the priests on Malabar Hill, promised them a large sum of money,

59
00:03:44.358 --> 00:03:46.499
and sent them to Calcutta on the next train.

60
00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:49.861
The priests had arrived ahead of Mr.

61
00:03:49.901 --> 00:03:54.724
Fogg and Passepartout, however, and Fix had been feverishly watching the train station in Calcutta.

62
00:03:55.616 --> 00:03:56.737
When Fix had seen Mr.

63
00:03:56.757 --> 00:04:01.660
Fogg and Passepartout disembark that morning, he had immediately found a policeman to arrest the men.

64
00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:08.224
Judge Obadiah had heard Passepartout's outburst about his shoes and said, "The facts are admitted.

65
00:04:09.304 --> 00:04:15.228
I condemn this man, Passepartout, to imprisonment for fifteen days and a fine of three hundred pounds.

66
00:04:16.729 --> 00:04:18.009
I further condemn Mr.

67
00:04:18.069 --> 00:04:22.252
Fogg to a week's imprisonment and a fine of one hundred and fifty pounds."

68
00:04:24.512 --> 00:04:26.373
Fix rubbed his hands with satisfaction.

69
00:04:27.573 --> 00:04:31.975
If Phileas Fogg could be detained in Calcutta for a week, it would be enough time for the

70
00:04:32.015 --> 00:04:33.656
arrest warrant to arrive from London.

71
00:04:35.197 --> 00:04:38.898
Phileas Fogg, as self-composed as ever, said, "I offer bail."

72
00:04:40.379 --> 00:04:41.720
Fix's blood ran cold.

73
00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:45.061
"You have that right," said the judge.

74
00:04:45.081 --> 00:04:48.703
"I will pay it at once," said Mr.

75
00:04:48.763 --> 00:04:52.976
Fogg, taking a roll of banknotes from the carpetbag.

76
00:04:52.996 --> 00:04:55.197
Bail was set at two thousand pounds, and Mr.

77
00:04:55.217 --> 00:04:56.698
Fogg handed the money to the clerk.

78
00:04:58.039 --> 00:05:02.061
"This sum will be restored to you at the end of your sentence," said the judge.

79
00:05:02.081 --> 00:05:06.163
"Meanwhile you are liberated on bail."

80
00:05:06.183 --> 00:05:09.885
"Come!" said Phileas Fogg to his servant.

81
00:05:11.326 --> 00:05:13.327
He offered his arm to Aouda and they departed.

82
00:05:14.728 --> 00:05:16.009
Fix followed them to the quay.

83
00:05:17.329 --> 00:05:21.532
His only hope was that Fogg would not be willing to leave the two thousand pounds behind him.

84
00:05:23.092 --> 00:05:25.594
The steamer departing for Hong Kong was the Rangoon.

85
00:05:27.416 --> 00:05:30.418
A flag flying from its mast signaled that it was about to depart.

86
00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:36.704
Fix watched as Fogg and his companions got out of their carriage and pushed off in a boat

87
00:05:36.744 --> 00:05:37.324
for the steamer.

88
00:05:39.066 --> 00:05:40.747
Fix stamped his feet in disappointment.

89
00:05:40.767 --> 00:05:43.650
"The rascal is off after all!"

90
00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:44.510
he said.

91
00:05:44.530 --> 00:05:47.693
"Two thousand pounds sacrificed!

92
00:05:48.554 --> 00:05:50.856
I'll follow him to the end of the world if necessary.

93
00:05:52.088 --> 00:05:56.230
But at the rate Fogg is going, the money he stole will soon be exhausted!"

94
00:06:00.212 --> 00:06:01.253
That was a close one!

95
00:06:02.173 --> 00:06:02.953
Thank goodness Mr.

96
00:06:02.993 --> 00:06:04.394
Fogg was able to post bail.

97
00:06:05.415 --> 00:06:06.455
But I must be careful.

98
00:06:07.776 --> 00:06:09.797
My mistakes are costing him so much money!

99
00:06:11.578 --> 00:06:16.180
I couldn't help noticing that Judge Obadiah's wig and clothes seemed royal, like something a king would wear.

100
00:06:17.352 --> 00:06:21.995
Interestingly, I learned that this costume does have links to royalty—French royalty!

101
00:06:23.275 --> 00:06:27.358
In the sixteenth century, King Louis XIII, of France, began wearing a wig.

102
00:06:28.618 --> 00:06:31.680
It didn't take long for all wealthy aristocrats to start wearing wigs too.

103
00:06:33.021 --> 00:06:36.683
However, by the eighteenth century, wigs became less and less popular.

104
00:06:37.903 --> 00:06:41.325
Today in England lawyers and judges are the only people who still wear them.

105
00:06:42.266 --> 00:06:43.186
And that's fine with me.

106
00:06:43.667 --> 00:06:45.247
I think I look perfect like this anyway!
